Decide a re-registration from the contact, not from the Via port.

The third and last place ipsec_create() asks which port the REGISTER came from.
Upstream 812df26470 already added the test that answers the question it is
really asking - the contact's ipsec_t still has port_pc/port_ps zero, which is
true exactly until the first ipsec_create() fills them in - but left the port
comparison in front of it as an ||, where it can only widen the first-
registration branch, never narrow it.

The sent-by port of a protected re-REGISTER is port_uc, and port_uc is the UE's
own choice, announced in its Security-Client header: nothing stops a UE picking
5060 for it, and a UE that does - or one that simply leaves its listening port
in the Via - takes the first-registration branch on every re-registration, on a
contact that already carries a complete tunnel.

The two branches differ in exactly two things. The first is old_s, which the
re-registration branch sets and this one does not, and which is the only way
update_contact_ipsec_params() ever reuses an installed tunnel. Without it the
CK/IK comparison has nothing to compare against and acquire_spi() hands out a
fresh SPI/port pair even when the vector did not change, so
ipsec_reuse_server_port - on by default - never fires for that UE. Patch 0003
then correctly tears the old tunnel down immediately before building the new
one, which means a registration refresh that should have been a no-op leaves
the UE with no tunnel for the width of that teardown, and names new protected
ports in the Security-Server of every 401.

The second is what happens to a re-REGISTER that carries no Security-Client.
The re-registration branch rejects it, and patch 0005 turns that into a 401
re-offering the tunnel that is installed. This branch instead takes
s = pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec - the contact's own struct - and
update_contact_ipsec_params() overwrites s->ck.s and s->ik.s with fresh shm
allocations without freeing what was there, then acquire_spi() overwrites
spi_pc/spi_ps/port_pc/port_ps in place. The installed tunnel's parameters are
now gone: patch 0003's guard is old_ipsec != s, false here, so nothing destroys
it, and destroy_ipsec_tunnel() at expiry reads the values that replaced it.
Eight SAs, eight policies and a slot out of ipsec_max_connections stay in the
kernel for good, plus the CK and IK buffers.

Dropping the port term leaves the condition that was already the correct one. A
first registration still takes the first-registration branch whatever port it
came from, because its contact holds the placeholder ipsec_t save_pending()
built from the Security-Client header, with the P-CSCF ports still zero.

diff --git a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
index c5224dc..75cd78d 100644
--- a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
+++ b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
@@ -913,10 +913,13 @@ int ipsec_create(struct sip_msg *m, udomain_t *d, int _cflags)
 	// Parse security parameters from the REGISTER request and get some data for the new tunnels
 	req_sec_params = cscf_get_security(req);
 
-	// Update contacts only for initial registration, for re-registration the existing contacts shouldn't be updated.
-	if(ci.via_port == SIP_PORT
-			|| (pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec->port_ps == 0
-					&& pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec->port_pc == 0)) {
+	// Whether a tunnel has to be built from scratch is a property of the
+	// contact, not of the port the REGISTER was sent from: until the first
+	// ipsec_create() fills it in, the contact carries the placeholder ipsec_t
+	// save_pending() built out of the Security-Client header, whose P-CSCF
+	// ports are still zero. That test alone decides it.
+	if(pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec->port_ps == 0
+			&& pcontact->security_temp->data.ipsec->port_pc == 0) {
 		LM_DBG("Registration for contact with AOR [%.*s], VIA [%d://%.*s:%d], "
 			   "received_host [%d://%.*s:%d]\n",
 				ci.aor.len, ci.aor.s, ci.via_prot, ci.via_host.len,
